title: Nokia ISAM: GPON port status, SFP optics and traffic
agents: snmp
catalog: hw/network/alcatel
license: GPLv3
distribution: check_mk
description:
 Monitors the GPON ports of a Nokia ISAM OLT. One service per line-card port.

 The service state compares the port's administrative and operational status:
 {OK} when they agree, {UNKNOWN} when the operational status is "unknown",
 {CRIT} otherwise.

 Metrics: SFP optics (Tx power, Rx power, supply voltage, temperature, bias
 current), traffic rates in bytes per second for unicast, multicast, broadcast
 and total in both directions, the matching drop rates, utilization against the
 ITU-T G.984 line rates (2.488 Gbit/s downstream, 1.244 Gbit/s upstream), the
 number of active ONTs and the DBA congestion time.

 Optics values the device reports as "not-available" are skipped rather than
 recorded as zero. On the OLT side Rx power is normally not available per PON
 port, so that metric is usually absent.

 Traffic rates are derived from the performance-monitoring current-interval
 counters, which the device clears every 300 seconds, one to three seconds
 after the wall-clock boundary. The check adapts to how often it is polled: at
 a short interval it differences consecutive readings, using the reading
 timestamps against the known cycle boundaries to decide whether a clear fell
 between them; at an interval close to the window length it instead divides the
 counter by the time elapsed since the window boundary. Rates above the
 physical line rate are discarded as impossible.

 This plugin provides two SNMP sections. {oposs_isam_gpon} carries the
 performance-monitoring counters and is cheap, about one second per host.
 {oposs_isam_gpon_info} carries the SFP optics inventory, the port descriptions
 and the interface status; it is expensive, because the optics table is read
 from the SFP hardware on demand, and it accounts for roughly half of the whole
 host's SNMP fetch time.

 The split exists so a short check interval stays affordable. Measured on a
 16-port OLT, per 300 seconds of wall time:

 300 s check interval: 18 s of SNMP. 60 s check interval without caching: 68 s,
 3.8 times as much. 60 s check interval with {oposs_isam_gpon_info} cached at
 300 s: 22 s, 1.2 times as much.

 To use a 60 second interval, set "Normal check interval for service checks"
 to one minute for these hosts (scoping the rule to the service {Check_MK$} is
 enough, since that is what drives the SNMP fetch), and add
 {oposs_isam_gpon_info} and {oposs_isam_linecard} to a "Fetch intervals for
 SNMP sections" rule at 300 seconds. Do not add {oposs_isam_gpon} there.

 The plugin also works unchanged at the default interval; the shorter interval
 mainly improves how faithfully bursty traffic is represented.

 Note for upgrades to 0.5.0: the traffic rate and utilization metrics were
 renamed, without a translation, so their graphs start empty. Every value they
 held was written by 0.2.0 through 0.4.0, whose rate conversion assumed a
 900 second window where the device uses 300, producing spikes far above the
 line rate and under-reporting steady traffic. The optics metrics and the ONT
 counters keep their history.

item:
 The port path as reported by the device, for example {lt:1/1/1/01}.

discovery:
 One service is created for each line-card port, that is every item whose path
 starts with {lt:}.
